What is Gastrointestinal Endoscopy?
Gastrointestinal endoscopy refers to a suite of minimally invasive procedures that allow physicians to examine the digestive tract using a flexible tube equipped with a light and camera. These procedures have become indispensable for both diagnostic and therapeutic purposes, enabling the detection and treatment of conditions ranging from ulcers and polyps to gastrointestinal cancers. The technology has evolved significantly from traditional scopes to include advanced modalities like capsule endoscopy and endoscopic ultrasound, offering patients safer alternatives to exploratory surgeries.
